# _KDB Object

`_KDB` is the central runtime registry for shared state, runtime resources, public module APIs, services, events, host references and project configuration.

Feature code receives `_KDB` as a function argument. Feature code does not read directly from `window._KDB`. For console or debug access use:

```js
getKDB();
```

Use `window.getKDB()`, `window.top.getKDB()` or `globalThis.getKDB()` only when explicitly debugging global publishing or host context.

## Allowed Top-Level Namespaces

```js
_KDB.state
_KDB.runtime
_KDB.services
_KDB.api
_KDB.events
_KDB.config
_KDB.properties
_KDB.elements
_KDB.utils
_KDB.cognosAPI
_KDB.data
```

A new top-level namespace is a structural change. Ask the user before introducing one.

## Forbidden Legacy Namespaces

```js
_KDB.utilities
_KDB.generalFunctions
_KDB.dropdownOptions
```

New code and ported code must not use these names. When porting legacy code, migrate utility functions to `_KDB.utils` or to the owning module's public API, and migrate dropdown/option lists to `_KDB.config.options`. Keeping a legacy name requires an explicit compatibility reason documented in the porting commit or contract; otherwise the migration is part of the port.

## Namespace Ownership Map

- `_KDB.state` — serializable dashboard/session state; one canonical writer per subtree. See `state.mdc`.
- `_KDB.runtime` — technical runtime resources per owner: listeners, subscriptions, abort controllers, observers, timers, live DOM and jQuery references, host objects, adapter instances, pending Promise references. See `runtime.mdc`.
- `_KDB.events` — the in-app event bus itself: `emit`, `subscribe`, `unsubscribe`, plus event-name constants. The bus is its own top-level namespace and is not a service. See `events.mdc`.
- `_KDB.services` — higher-level shared infrastructure that consumes wrappers and the event bus, with its own coordination or state (history, table data, logging, cognosHost). Services may use `_KDB.events`. Services must not consume `_KDB.cognosAPI` except the cognosHost service (ring 1); other services consume injected `_KDB.services.cognosHost` facets (or nothing). The event bus itself does not live under services. See `module-public-api.mdc`.
- `_KDB.api` — public module APIs for feature modules (`_KDB.api.<module>`). See `module-public-api.mdc`.
- `_KDB.cognosAPI` — thin Cognos host wrappers (inner ring / host-wrapper knowledge base), always thematic shape `_KDB.cognosAPI.<theme>.<function>`. One host-chain per wrapper, no feature flow, no state mirroring. Only the cognosHost service may consume these wrappers; wrappers do not consume services.
- `_KDB.config` — declarative project config, labels, language values and available option lists. Dropdown/option lists live under `_KDB.config.options`.
- `_KDB.properties` — Cognos dashboard spec / property panel configuration; not runtime state.
- `_KDB.elements` — selector strings and selector configuration only. New selector configuration lives under `_KDB.elements.selectors.<module>`. Live DOM nodes, jQuery objects and host-element references belong in `_KDB.runtime.<owner>`. The legacy `_KDB.elements.kdbControl` and `_KDB.elements.kdbWidget` host references are documented exceptions for existing code; they may not be reused as selector namespaces, and the pattern is not copied to new modules. See `dom.mdc`.
- `_KDB.utils` — pure utility functions, published per `module-public-api.mdc`.
- `_KDB.data` — shared read-only data caches that are not owned by a service lifecycle, for example static hierarchies or dashboard-wide reference data loaded once. Loaded table rows owned by `tableDataService` live under `_KDB.state.tableData`, not under `_KDB.data`. If a candidate value has a clear owning service, it belongs to that service's state subtree.

## State vs Runtime vs Config

- Selected runtime values such as filter selections or active tab go in `_KDB.state`.
- DOM nodes, listeners, observers, timers, abort controllers, Promise objects, Cognos API objects and adapter instances go in `_KDB.runtime.<owner>`.
- Declarative options such as dropdown lists or labels go in `_KDB.config`; option lists live under `_KDB.config.options`.
- Cognos host configuration values go in `_KDB.properties`.

A value that crosses these categories is a sign that the categorisation is wrong, not that the namespaces should merge.

## Discipline

- Do not use `_KDB` as a shortcut to avoid AMD imports. Internal code dependencies go through AMD imports; shared runtime APIs go through the allowed `_KDB` namespaces.
- Do not replace an entire namespace from feature code. Mutations go through canonical writers, orchestrators or public core APIs.
- Do not serialize or persist `_KDB` as a whole. Only explicitly designed state subtrees may be restored or written back.
- Do not introduce ad hoc top-level keys such as `_KDB.cache`, `_KDB.tmp` or `_KDB.shared`.

## When AI Must Ask

The AI must explicitly ask the user when:

- a new top-level `_KDB.*` namespace appears necessary;
- a value does not fit cleanly under any existing namespace (likely indicates miscategorisation);
- legacy code is reusing a forbidden namespace name and a renamed home is unclear.

Ask-template: *"For value `<X>` I propose namespace `<Y>` because `<reason>`. Acceptable, or should we discuss placement?"*

